# Dev Feedback Loop

After any UI-affecting code change, use this workflow to visually verify the result.

## Quick Reference

```bash
make run-bg          # Start app in background (writes .dev/app.pid)
make wait-window     # Poll until the app window appears (up to 60s)
make screenshot      # Capture the app window → screenshots/latest.png
make stop            # Kill the tracked instance and clean up .dev/
make dev-feedback    # All-in-one: stop → run-bg → wait-window → screenshot
```

## Full Workflow

1. **Start the app** (skip if already running):
   ```
   Shell: make run-bg
   ```
   Wait for output confirming the PID was detected.

2. **Wait for the window**:
   ```
   Shell: make wait-window
   ```
   This polls every 2s for up to 60s until the Compose window exists.

3. **Take a screenshot**:
   ```
   Shell: make screenshot
   ```
   Saves to `screenshots/latest.png` and `screenshots/history/<timestamp>.png`.

4. **Read and analyze the screenshot**:
   ```
   Read: screenshots/latest.png
   ```
   Cursor's Read tool natively renders PNG images for visual inspection.

5. **If changes are needed**: stop, edit code, and repeat:
   ```
   Shell: make stop
   ```
   Then edit code and go back to step 1.

## Iteration Comparison

Previous screenshots are kept in `screenshots/history/`. To compare before
and after, read both the current `latest.png` and the relevant history file.

## One-Time Setup: Screen Recording Permission

The screenshot step requires macOS Screen Recording permission for Cursor.
If `make screenshot` fails with "Screen Recording permission required", tell the
user to grant it:

> System Settings → Privacy & Security → Screen Recording → enable **Cursor**

The script will auto-open System Settings on first failure. After granting,
Cursor may need a restart.

## Important Notes

- `make run-bg` uses instance stamping — it only tracks/kills the exact JVM
  process it started, so it is safe even if other Java processes are running.
- First launch after a full rebuild takes ~1.5 min; incremental ~12s.
- The first run on macOS may crash with a MetalRedrawer error — just run again.
- If `make wait-window` times out, check `.dev/app.log` for startup errors.
- The Quartz Python dependency is auto-installed in `.dev/venv/` on first use.
